Problem Statement
Given an array of integers, write a program to find the sums of all possible subsets of the array. 
The sums can be returned in any order.

Examples
Example 1:

Input: arr = [2, 3]

Output: [0, 2, 3, 5]

Explanation: The subsets of the given array are [], [2], [3], and [2, 3]. 
The corresponding sums of these subsets are 0, 2, 3, and 5.

Example 2:

Input: arr = [5, 2, 1]

Output: [0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8]

Explanation: The subsets are [], [1], [2], [1, 2], [5], [1, 5], [2, 5], and [1, 2, 5]. 
Summing the elements of each subset yields the output above.

Bitmasking Approach (Iterative) 
In this approach we use binary numbers to represent subsets. For an array of size N 
ther are exactly 2^N subsets. 
We can iterate from 0 to (2^N) - 1
If the jth bit of a number i is set(that is 1) we include arr[j] in the current subset sum.

class Solution {
public: 
    vector<int> subsetSums(vector<int> arr, int n) {
        vector<int> ans;
        int totalSubsets = 1 << n; //Equivalent to 2^n

        //Iterate through all possible subsets using numbers from 0 to 2^n-1
        for (int i = 0; i < totalSubsets; i++) {
            int currentSum = 0;
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                //Check if jth bit is set in i 
                if (i & (1 << j)) {
                    currentSum += arr[j];
                }
            }
            ans.push_back(currentSum);
        }

        //Optional: Sort the answer if output need to be strictly increasing 
        //sort(ans.begin(), ans.end())
        return ans;
    }
}

TC: O(N*2^N)
The outer loop runs 2^N times.
Inner loop checks exactly N bits for each subset.

SC: O(2^N)
For storing all the subset sums in the ans array.


2. Recursive Approach (Pick and Non-Pick)
This is the classic backtracking approach. 
At every index, we make two recursive calls: one where we pick the current element (add it to our sum) and 
one where we do not pick the current element. Once we reach the end of the array, we push the collected sum into our answer list.

class Solution {
private:
    void solve(int ind, int sum, vector<int>& arr, int n, vector<int>& ans) {
        // Base case: if we have traversed the entire array
        if (ind == n) {
            ans.push_back(sum);
            return;
        }
        
        // Choice 1: Pick the element at the current index
        solve(ind + 1, sum + arr[ind], arr, n, ans);
        
        // Choice 2: Do not pick the element
        solve(ind + 1, sum, arr, n, ans);
    }

public:
    vector<int> subsetSums(vector<int> arr, int n) {
        vector<int> ans;
        // Start the recursion from index 0 with an initial sum of 0
        solve(0, 0, arr, n, ans);
        
        // sort(ans.begin(), ans.end());
        return ans;
    }
};

TC: O(2^N)
Since we branch out into two possibilities at each of the N steps, there are exactly 2^N recursive calls made.
This is faster than the bitmasking approach coz we maintain a running sum instead of iterating N time sto recalculate it.

SC: O(2^N)
To store the array of results.
Plus O(N) auxiliary stack space used by recursion tree (the maximum depth of the call stack is N)

